II. RAMBIN'S CONTEMPT AFFIDAVIT — FOUR INDEPENDENT DEFECTS
Defect 1: Internal Statutory Contradiction — Fatal on the Face of the Document
The affidavit's opening paragraph states it is "for the purpose of securing an arrest warrant" to establish probable cause that "ORS 163.732, Stalking and ORS 166.090, Telephonic Harassment has been violated," but then states "I have probable cause to believe that Ashle Antoinette Penn, committed the crime of ORS 135.290 Contempt of court."
The affidavit announces it will establish probable cause for Stalking and Telephonic Harassment. It never does. The entire body of the affidavit addresses only the contempt theory. No facts in the affidavit support stalking or telephonic harassment. The accompanying misdemeanor complaint charges all three offenses but the supporting affidavit establishes probable cause for none of the stalking or harassment charges. This is a charging instrument sufficiency defect going to the face of the arrest warrant itself.
Assessment: Strong. An arrest warrant supported by an affidavit that fails to establish probable cause for the charged offenses violates the Fourth Amendment particularity requirement.

Defect 2: Rambin States the Wrong Legal Element — The Statutory Collapse You Already Identified
Rambin states: "A person commits the crime of ORS 135.290 Punishment by contempt of court if the defendant breaches any of the regulations in the release agreement imposed pursuant to ORS 135.260 (Conditional Release)."
Rambin has correctly identified the statutory hook — ORS 135.290 requires a violation of a conditional release under ORS 135.260. His own element statement therefore makes the nature of AshLe's release a dispositive question. If AshLe's release was a security release (money bail under ORS 135.265), not a conditional release under ORS 135.260, then by Rambin's own stated framework, no ORS 135.290 contempt occurred. The affidavit never identifies which type of release was imposed, never cites the release agreement language specifying it as a conditional release under ORS 135.260, and never quotes the specific condition allegedly violated.
Rule: ORS 135.290 authorizes contempt only for violations of ORS 135.260 conditional release or ORS 135.247 contact orders in sex crime or domestic violence cases. Neither applies to a security release on theft and drug charges.
Delta: The affidavit's own element statement concedes the statutory requirement but fails to establish that AshLe's release met it.
Assessment: Strong. The contempt charge fails on the face of the affidavit under Rambin's own stated legal framework.

Defect 3: Staleness — Seven Months Between Alleged Violation and Warrant
The affidavit states the alleged violation occurred on May 12, 2025 — AshLe's presence at Barry Washington's arrest — but was sworn before a notary on December 29, 2025.
That is approximately seven months between the alleged conduct and the warrant application. Oregon courts evaluate staleness based on the nature of the alleged violation and whether the supporting facts are stale by the time the warrant issues. A contempt charge based on alleged contact at a specific location on a specific date in May, supported by police presence and body camera footage that existed contemporaneously, does not require a seven-month investigative delay. The delay is unexplained in the affidavit.
